AB  - Sleep disorders have become a pressing global health concern, necessitating the development of effective and non-pharmacological neuromodulation strategies. In this work, a brain functional network-based sleep modulation framework is proposed using transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation (TENS) applied to the greater occipital nerve. The stimulation protocol employs a spindle-like waveform pattern composed of superimposed sinusoidal signals to enhance low-frequency brain rhythms associated with sleep regulation. To evaluate TENS effects on insomnia, electroencephalography (EEG) data were collected from thirty participants under sham and stimulation conditions in a controlled experimental study. Results reveal that TENS significantly increases phase-locking value (PLV) in the delta and theta bands. Furthermore, graph-theoretical analysis shows marked enhancements in brain network topology under stimulation. Correlation analyses indicate that improvements in network metrics, particularly in the delta band, are associated with better subjective sleep quality (PSQI scores). The most significant changes are observed in frontal and occipital regions, indicating improved long-range communication and integrative processing ability. The results demonstrate that TENS modulates functional connectivity and enhances sleep quality, offering a promising non-pharmacological intervention for insomnia.
